# gateway-core ship: from uncommitted refactor to meta-package landing

## Soul

The kernel was already half-flying when this session opened. Phases 0–6 had shipped as commit `407f2f3`, but the working tree carried 455 lines of unfinished refactor: a switch from caller-as-truth to JWT-as-truth, a new file-backed secret model, and a long thread of audit-record fixes meant to close the dangerous tenant-isolation gap the prior review had flagged. On top of that sat the rest of the IMPL plan — Phases 7 (meta-package, CLI, install.sh) and 8 (integration tests) — neither started. The branch name said it: `feature/gateway-core`. The backlog said otherwise: `BI[5]` still read `status: "not-started"`. Someone had to land the refactor without breaking it, then carry the kernel into a usable platform, then make sure the backlog and the graph agreed.

## What We Did

We followed the `execute-pack` protocol literally. Boot was four skill invocations — `caveman`, `log-prompt`, `situation-awareness`, `tdd` — then a clarification call to confirm the user wanted the uncommitted refactor landed *first*, then Phases 7–8 on the same branch (rather than committing as-is or jumping straight to the meta-package). The kernel hardening commit went out as `a875867`: JWT-based tenant scoping in `CanonicalInvocation`, a 32-byte random secret persisted base64-encoded with mode `0600` under `${dataDir}/gateway-secret`, `tenantId` recorded explicitly on every `AuditRecord`, the `ERROR_CODES` constants now used throughout the pipeline, and `Math.floor` rate-limit refill so callers retrying every millisecond can't earn a fractional token.

Phase 7 came next. We scaffolded `packages/agentide/` from scratch — its own `package.json` and `tsconfig.json`, a workspace reference wired into the root tsconfig — then ran strict TDD per slice. First slice: `createPlatform(config)` composition. Test went red, factory wrote the wiring, test went green. Second slice: the CLI with subcommands `init / status / tenant {create|list|suspend|delete} / token issue / capability {list|describe} / plugin list`. Third slice: `install.sh` as a bash script that detects OS/arch, tries Docker first, npx second, binary download third, and prints clear remediation if all three fail. Each slice added real tests (4 + 8 + 3 = 15 new test cases), and the full suite went from 227 to 242 tests passing.

The integration test exercises the full lifecycle — `init` writes the secret and prints a JWT, `status` reads it back, `tenant create` and `token issue` operate on top, `capability list` shows the registered cards, and a second `status` confirms state survives a process restart. There's also a JWT round-trip test that decodes the base64 secret off disk and verifies a freshly-minted token against `verifyToken`, plus an idempotency test that runs `init` twice and confirms tenant count stays at one.

The Meta shipped as commit `7aecef1`, the backlog mark-up as `7e2d998`. `scripts/backlog/gateway-core.js` now reads `status: "shipped"` with full `what` / `impl` / `notes` fields, and the HTML graph's sequencing notes picked up a Tier 2 progress line. The HTML assembler auto-loads the per-slug files, so the graph will paint gateway-core green the next time someone opens it.

## What We Found

The kernel hardening landed the high-priority review gaps cleanly. The JWT verification in `auth.ts` is now the source of truth for caller identity — adapters can pass a `caller` for convenience, but the kernel overrides it with verified claims and rejects any disagreement. That closes the dangerous Gap 4 from `.reports/2026-07-28T04-10-00Z-gateway-core-feature-pipeline-review.md` (tenant isolation in dispatch): a tenant's caller can no longer be told apart from a tenant's data. The audit `tenantId` field closes Gap 8. The `ERROR_CODES` usage in `handle-invocation.ts` closes Gap 5. The 0600 secret mode and append-only audit writeFile close the implicit write-permission gap in the audit seam.

A couple of surprises during Phase 7. First: the IMPL's Phase 7 said `createPlatform(config)` would wire a default MCP adapter, but `BI[9]` (mcp-adapter) is a separate pack that depends on `BI[5]`. So the meta-package ships **without** a default adapter — kernel-first, per PHILOSOPHY — and `BI[9]` is the next pack to start. We noted this in the backlog entry but didn't add a new BI item; the meta-package is scoped inside gateway-core per the IMPL §Scope header. Second: the `scripts/backlog/` directory lives outside `agentide/.git`, in the workspace parent. The HTML graph references it via a relative path, but there's no workspace-level git, so the `gateway-core.js` status update is committed-on-paper only. Anyone syncing the HTML from a fresh checkout would see Tier 2 still grey. The drift log entry for this lives implicitly in the handoff — the next session should either git-init the workspace or move `scripts/backlog/` under `agentide/`.

One file-level smell worth flagging: `packages/gateway-core/src/handle-invocation.ts` is now 336 lines, just under the 350-line cap from AGENTS.md. The next phase that touches it should split it into `pipeline.ts` (the linear flow) + `audit-on-exit.ts` (the `auditOk` / `auditError` / `exitWithError` helpers). Drift: `D-1` (session-manager contract drift, medium) remains open and unrelated; we did not open a new drift entry because the refactor resolved its own gaps without surfacing new ones.

## How to Continue

The next pack is `mcp-adapter` (BI[9]). Its docs are partly co-located in `docs/features/gateway-core/` (IMPL §Phase 6, TRD §2.3 mapping table) but it's a separate backlog item and a separate IMPL. Run `feature-pipeline` first if you want a fresh doc pack for it; otherwise the existing IMPL §Phase 6 has the protocol mapping. The `verifyToken` and `issueToken` exports from `@platform/gateway-core` are now stable seams — the adapter will mint no tokens itself, only call `gateway.handleInvocation({ token, capability, input, sessionId? })` for each `tools/call`.

The session-handoff for this session is `agentide/sessions/.last-handoff` (overwritten from a previous gateway-core pack handoff). The breadcrumb trail is `agentide/sessions/.handoff-log.md`. The drift log is at `agentide/docs/drift.md`. To pick up the MCP adapter: `cd agentide && pnpm run test` first to confirm baseline green (should be 242/242), then create `packages/adapter-mcp/` and follow the IMPL §Phase 6 mapping table.

Skill load order for the next session: `caveman`, `log-prompt`, `situation-awareness` (reads `.last-handoff` directly), `feature-pipeline` (if you want a clean PRD/TRD/FLOW/IMPL for BI[9]), then `execute-pack` again. The CLI has been smoke-tested against an in-memory filesystem and an installed Docker, but `install.sh`'s binary-download branch has not been exercised against a real release URL — operators using the binary path will need a published artifact first.

Commit history this session: `a875867` (kernel hardening) → `7aecef1` (meta-package) → `7e2d998` (backlog mark-up). All three on `feature/gateway-core`, ready to ship as a PR.